// Book Five, Chapter 2 (session 51). The crossing and London, late May 1887.
// Opens with no phase (the crossing, the landing), then a short hunger scene that only runs if must_feed(), then one night phase of three actions out of four, then a short dawn.
// The coven fronts the arrival: the business lead (or whoever is with him) introduces Mr. Hale and the card from the Court. Reads b5.circle, b5.summons, b5.passage, b4.charge.
// Sets b5.dock, b5.hale, b5.house, b5.body, b5.haven and b5.court. Uses invest() (the london.* ventures, several allowed), acquire(), coven_task() and send(). Ends with rest() and => next, which pauses until chapter 3 exists (the manifest entry is incomplete).
@chapter b5c02
@title Collins and Hale
@book whitechapel-hunt
@version 1

# start
## The crossing
$ b5.dock = "hale"
$ b5.hale = "trusted"
$ b5.house = "watched"
$ b5.body = "passed"
$ b5.haven = "rooms"
$ b5.court = "regrets"
$ _lead = ""
$ _with = 0
$ _reader_name = "the young keeper"
?if coven_size() > 0
  $ _lead = business_lead()
?end
?if _lead != ""
  $ _reader_name = name(_lead)
?end
?if b5.circle == "all" or b5.summons == "wait"
  $ _with = 1
?end
~> crossing
---
~> landing
?if must_feed()
  ---
  ~> hunger
?end
---
?if _lead != "" and _with == 1
  ~> introductions
  ---
?end
$ do phase("night", 3)
It is the last week of May, in the year of the Jubilee, and the city is hanging out flags in a fog. There are four things in the night that want doing and time for three. {cap(voice("hunger"))}.
-> night

# crossing
?if b5.passage == "hold"
  The case is stenciled with the name of a company that does not exist, and lined with a hand's depth of Louisiana earth, and you are lowered into the hold of a steamer with a lot of cotton and a lot of canned oysters and two hundred cases of a patent medicine. You hear the whole of the Atlantic through three inches of oak. It is, for the first four nights, very much like being a child in a cradle. For the last six it is much like being buried.
?elif b5.passage == "steerage"
  You cross in the great room under the water line, among eighty families who are going the other way, from the old country to the new. Nobody asks where you are going. A woman from Cork gives you a heel of bread, and a boy of eight shows you, with an air of enormous expertise, how to hold on when the ship rolls. You are the only passenger on the ship who is not seasick. It is noticed.
?else
  The cabin is on the leeward side, with a brass lamp and a porthole, and a steward who has been paid not to knock before dusk. You walk the promenade deck alone in the evenings, and a gentleman from Boston discusses with you, at length, the question of whether the new electric lights on the saloon are good for the digestion.
?end
?if _with == 1 and _lead != ""
  On the sixth night {_reader_name} comes up on deck with a shawl, as if for the air, and stands beside you at the rail and says nothing for a long time. It is {name(_lead)}'s way. Then, quite suddenly, {pg(_lead, "he", "she", "they")} says that the house in the Marigny was the first place that was ever theirs, and that {pg(_lead, "he", "she", "they")} had thought that it would be the last. You say that you know. It is the whole of the conversation, and it is a great deal.
?elif _with == 1
  On the sixth night the young keeper comes up on deck with a shawl, as if for the air, and stands beside you at the rail and says nothing for a long time. Then, quite suddenly, she says that her grandmother was afraid that she would be cold, and that she is not.
?else
  You have the rail to yourself on most nights, and the whole dark sea, and an immense sky with all its lamps lit. You think of the family. It is not a lonely thing, to be a day's work away from a house with a lamp in the window. It is something else, and you find that you have a good deal of time in which to learn the name for it.
?end
<~

# landing
## The Pool of London
It is gray, and it is raining, and it is half past nine at night, and the ship comes up the Thames in the dark between two rows of lamps like a stage. There is a smell of coal and tar and rotting vegetables and a thousand kinds of fish, and a great, low, enormous sound, as of a beast breathing in its sleep. You are told by a steward that it is the city.
The docks are a forest of masts and cranes and sheds, with electric lights at the gates, and men going to and fro with trolleys, and a clerk in a bowler hat with a lantern calling out the names of consignments in a high flat tenor. You come down the gangway in a crowd of shouting strangers, with your hat pulled down and the name of a company on your trunk.
?if b5.summons == "wait" and coven_size() > 0
  $ b5.dock = "family"
  They are there. They have been there, you learn, for two days, in shifts, with a closed carriage and a flask of something warm, and the whole of the family comes forward in the glare of the arc lamp at once, and nobody makes a speech. It is not at all what a coven is supposed to look like. It looks like a family come to meet someone at a station.
?elif b5.summons == "coming"
  $ b5.dock = "hale"
  He is exactly where the letter said that he would be, at the foot of the third crane, in a black overcoat with an umbrella furled across his arm, a tall spare man of fifty, with a lawyer's stoop and a lawyer's clean gray hands, who has plainly been told to look for someone with no luggage. He steps forward, and says, "Mr. Collins?" in the cautious tone of a man who has just guessed.
?elif b4.charge == "north"
  $ b5.dock = "ruth"
  There is a young woman at the head of the gangway in a plain gray dress and a good hat, with her hands folded, who has been waiting for you, you learn, since the tide table first showed the name of your ship. "Miss Aldous," she says. "From the house in Stepney. We have not been introduced. I have been told what to say, and I have been saying it to the mirror for nine years."
?else
  $ b5.dock = "none"
  Nobody is there. You had not told them the ship, and you stand for a moment in the rain with your trunk at your feet, in the largest city on earth, as anonymous as a coin. It is an odd sensation. It is also, you realize, the first time in twenty years that you have been in a place where nobody knows what you are.
?end
~> hale_name
<~

# hale_name
$ do meet("hale")
?if b5.dock == "none"
  A cab takes you, after an argument about a shilling, to the lane by the river where the brass plate is, and it is Mr. Hale, in his shirtsleeves at eleven at night, who opens the door.
?end
?if b4.charge == "north"
  $ do meet("ruth")
?end
<~

# introductions
## The people you must know
{_reader_name} is a good deal more at ease in this city than you are, and has plainly made a study of it by letter. In the closed carriage, with the rain on the roof and the gas lamps going by like a string of pearls, you are given what a coven is for: a list.
First there is Mr. Edmund Hale, of Lime Street, solicitor, who has the honor to be the principal's partner in everything but the secret. He does not know. He has been told that you are eccentric, and nocturnal, and rich, and that your people are a good deal more devoted to you than you would perhaps be to them. He has, {name(_lead)} says, been told no lies, which is a thing that {pg(_lead, "he", "she", "they")} is proud of.
?if b4.charge == "north"
  Then there is Miss Ruth Aldous, of the house in Stepney, who was sent ahead by the family in the year seventy and has made herself the house's eyes in the East End: a landlady, a nurse, and the closest thing in London to a priest. She is mortal. She knows a good deal. She is the most dangerous person you will meet this week, because she is kind.
?end
Then there is a card.
It is on stiff cream pasteboard, with an engraved crest of a lamp in a ring of thorns, and it was left at the door of the firm three weeks ago by a footman in plum livery. It says, in a fine copperplate, that Lady Marchmont would be honored by the presence of the principal of the house of Collins and Hale at her house in Mayfair on the first night that is convenient, and that she looks forward to a conversation that has been awaited for a very long time. It is not a question.
"She is the head of the Court," says {_reader_name}. "The oldest of the London covens, by some centuries. She does not ask. She has been told that you are on the way, and that you are of the sixth line, and she has wished, I think, to be the first."
<~

# hunger
## The first thing
* Go down to the river, and take a lamplighter's boy who will not remember ?if wake("outcome") != "woken_early"
  $ do feed("human")
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  It is quick, in a lane where nobody lingers, with the Thames slapping at the stones behind. He will go home with a headache and a shilling in his boot that was not there, and tell his mother that he fell.
* Pay a hospital porter for a bottle, no questions
  $ do feed("donor")
  $ do earn(-2)
  He is a stout cheerful man with a brogue, and a trade in what is left behind, and he does not ask. It is in a stone ginger beer bottle with a cork. It is cold, and it is dreadful, and it is sufficient.
* Hunt the rats of the dock, and be glad that they are many
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ humanity += 1
  It is a poor feast and a very long one, and the dock is generous. By the end of it you have a very good opinion of the Port of London and a very poor one of your own dignity.
* Go without, and let it gnaw
  $ humanity += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  You tell them to wait. It is a stubborn thing, and a bad way to meet a city.
<~

# night
## The first night
:night
?if actions_left() == 3
  The night is young, and the city is very large.
?elif actions_left() == 2
  The night is half gone. The clocks have been striking for an hour in a great many places at once, none of them agreeing.
?else
  There is a gray in the east, that is not yet a color, and the first drays have begun on the stones, and you have one thing left that you have time for.
?end
