# firm
## Lime Street
$ do discover("the_city_of_london")
It is a narrow house of soot colored brick, between a marine insurer and a tea merchant, with a brass plate that has been polished every day for seven years by someone who does not know why. Mr. Hale lights the gas himself, and brings out three ledgers and a decanter of sherry that he does not drink, and sits down on the far side of a partners' desk with the look of a man who has rehearsed this for a month.
"I shall be frank, Mr. Collins," he says. "I have kept this account for seven years and I have never met the principal. I have taken the family's word that he exists, and I have taken the bank's word that he pays. I find myself, now, very much hoping that I was right."
:the_hale
* Say that he was, and thank him, and sign what he has laid out
  $ b5.hale = "trusted"
  $ standing += 2
  He signs the next page, with a hand that is trembling very slightly, and does not look up for some time. "I have not had so good a night in seven years," he says. "I wish my father could have seen it."
* Say that you will take his word, and ask what he has not written down
  $ b5.hale = "tested"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  He is quiet. Then he says that there is one thing, a small thing, a client who is not a client, a gentleman of the old sort with a white face, who comes on the first Tuesday of every month and leaves a card and goes away. He has kept the cards. There are eighty four of them. The gentleman has never once signed.
:the_venture
Hale has three things to be decided, he says, and he lays them out in a row on the green leather like a man dealing a hand. He has a cautious heart and a keen eye. The city, he says, has more money in it than any place on earth, and a great deal of it is in motion.
* Put money into shipping, the long boats and the warehouses of the river [once] ?if can_invest("london.shipping")
  $ do invest("london.shipping")
  $ standing += 1
  Four ships, and a quarter share in a fifth, and a wharf at Wapping with a crane. It is a good plain thing. It also gives the firm what it wants most of all, which is a reason to have a dock with a lamp on it at all hours, where a case can be unloaded on any night without a question.
  -> :the_venture
* Take a share in the railways, the new lines to the coast and the North [once] ?if can_invest("london.railways")
  $ do invest("london.railways")
  $ standing += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  It is the great fortune of the age, and a plain one. Hale shows you a map of the lines, with the new ones in red like veins, and you find to your surprise that you are in love with it. It is a very large machine, and you have been looking at large machines for a thousand years.
  -> :the_venture
* Take a stake in the underwriting rooms, and the insurance of the ships [once] ?if can_invest("london.insurance")
  $ do invest("london.insurance")
  $ standing += 2
  It is the business of every fear that has ever sailed out of a harbor, and the oldest game in the City. You sign a policy that insures a cargo of tea against the loss of the ship, and you reflect that it is very much the same thing that you do for the family.
  -> :the_venture
* Supply the hospitals, with bandages, carbolic and glass [once] ?if can_invest("london.medical")
  $ do invest("london.medical")
  $ humanity += 1
  $ do discover("the_east_end_hospitals")
  It pays very little, and you are not sorry. The London Hospital is a mile east, with a thousand beds and a queue at its door every morning of the year, and it will have the best carbolic in England by the end of the month.
  -> :the_venture
* Take the opium, which pays the most, and ask no more [once] ?if can_invest("london.opium")
  $ do invest("london.opium")
  $ humanity -= 5
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ do discover("the_opium_trade")
  Hale does not look up. It is the one of the lot that is a business of the dock and the dark, and the poppies, and the houses by the river where men lie down on benches and do not rise. It pays more than the rest together. You sign, and the ink dries, and you have a share of the Limehouse trade that you will have to answer for.
  -> :the_venture
* That will do for tonight
-> firm_end

# firm_end
<~

# east
## The streets of the East
$ do discover("whitechapel")
You walk. It is the only way to learn a city, and you have always been very good at it. You come out of Aldgate under a great pale arch of electric lamps, and the gas stops, and the other thing begins.
It is a city of streets that are not streets: lanes between lines of houses so close that the eaves nearly meet, with a gutter that runs in the middle, and doorways, and yards, and steps leading down into rooms that are not rooms. There are cabinet makers, and tailors, and men with barrows, and a great many small shops with the signs in a language that you have never seen written. There are children on the pavement at midnight, in flocks. There are women in the doorways, young and old, with an expression that you have seen on the faces of the dead.
The lodging houses have their names painted over the doors, and their prices: fourpence the bed, twopence the rope. You pass a man asleep on his feet, leaning on a line stretched across a room. It is cheaper, you learn from a boy, than lying down.
A woman in a doorway says something to you in a voice that has been used too much. A gentleman goes by in a silk hat, to somewhere he should not be, and does not look at her at all. In a hundred yards you have counted three hundred and forty people, and not one who looks at the sky.
~> cassian_house
<~

# cassian_house
## The address in Spitalfields
The house is the fourth on a lane, with a green door, and a window with a flowerpot in it, and the number that was at the head of a letter. The door is not locked. It is a thing that a man of his age would never do.
Inside there are two rooms, very clean, with a table laid for two, a cold pot of tea, and a lamp that has been burning for a week and gone out. There is a bookcase with eleven books on it in four languages. On the mantel, in a frame, there is a lock of hair tied with a ribbon, and a slip of paper in a hand that you know.
> *Wait for me. Do not go east of Aldgate alone.*
A neighbor is on the stair, a gray, quick, stringy woman with her hands under her apron and a face like a kind hatchet. She says that he went out on the Thursday, and that he said he would be back by supper, and that he has been a very good lodger for four years, though he does keep very odd hours. She says that a gentleman came in a closed carriage the Friday after and went through the rooms, and did not take anything, and left the door as you see it.
:the_house
* Go through the rooms yourself, and be thorough
  $ b5.house = "entered"
  $ do witness(1)
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  It takes the better part of an hour. You are not looking for anything in particular, and so you find it: a hollow in the wall behind the bookcase, a pen and a bottle of red ink, a book with the leaves cut out and a plan of the streets of Whitechapel drawn in the hollow, marked with eleven small crosses in a hand that is not his. The crosses are in a pattern. You do not yet know what the pattern is for.
* Wait in the dark, and watch the lane
  $ b5.house = "watched"
  $ secrecy += 1
  You wait. It is a very quiet street. For two hours nothing happens, and at the third a man goes by in a gray overcoat, and looks up at the window, and goes on, and you have the odd, cold sense of having been counted. You are quite sure that you were meant to feel it.
* Take the neighbor aside, kindly, and ask what she has seen
  $ b5.house = "spoke"
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  She tells you, in the end, as people do who have been listened to, a good deal more than she meant. A girl who used to sell flowers at the corner is gone. Another has not been seen at the lodging house since the week before Easter. They say that it is the drink, and the Lord, and the gin. She says that it is not.
~> body
<~

# body
## A doorway
It is nearly three when you hear the whistle. It comes from the end of the lane, a high, hysterical, ragged sound, and then another, and a heavy boot on the stones. By the time that you have come round the corner there is a small knot of people at the mouth of a court, and a constable with a bull's eye lantern, who is a very young man and quite white.
She is a woman of thirty or perhaps of forty, who looks, in the light of the lantern, like a tired child. She lies in a doorway in a gray shawl, with her hands folded, and there is no blood on the stones, and there is no wound that you can see. Her face is perfectly peaceful. She is as white as a candle.
The constable says that she is the third that he has had this month, and that the surgeon says that it is her heart.
It is not her heart. You know, before you have bent down, what it is. There is a small mark behind the ear, healed over, and under it a place where the blood has been taken, neatly, and with great skill, by someone who did not enjoy it.
:the_body
* Kneel by her, and look closely
  $ b5.body = "looked"
  $ do witness(1)
  $ humanity += 1
  You kneel in the wet, with the constable's lantern at your shoulder, and you look. It is the work of one of your own kind, you are quite certain, and it is not done in hunger. It is done with an instrument. The wound has been closed afterward, tenderly, with a thing like a stitch of silk. It is not an act of appetite. It is an act of care. It makes you angrier than anything you have yet seen.
* Walk by, and leave her to the constable
  $ b5.body = "passed"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ humanity -= 1
  It is the careful thing, and you do it, and it is the first time in your long life that you have walked past a body in the dark and been ashamed of it before you had reached the end of the street. You tell yourself that you will come back. You do not tell yourself when.
* Send a message to the house, to see to her decently ?if coven_size() > 0
  $ b5.body = "sent"
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  It is a small thing, and it is not nothing. By morning there is a decent coffin and a priest and a notice in the parish register, all paid for by an account that nobody can trace, and a family in Bethnal Green has been told that their daughter is to be buried with her name.
<~

# roof
## A roof
You have to sleep somewhere, and the family must be put somewhere, and the choice, in this city, is a matter of money and also of memory. The firm has been looking for twenty years. It has found three.
:the_haven
* A warehouse by the river at Wapping, with a cellar to the water, and a dock of one's own
  $ b5.haven = "warehouse"
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  It has a cellar that goes down below the tide line, behind a door that is not on the plan, and a crane on the roof, and a smell that discourages visitors. It is as safe as anything in London can be. It is also, you reflect, a place that no family would ever call home, and you tell yourself that you will think of something.
* A tall house in Stepney, with a garden at the back and a view of the whole of the East ?if b4.charge == "north" or coven_size() > 0
  $ b5.haven = "house"
  $ standing += 1
  It is a tall, narrow, dark red house, with a lamp over the door, a very old pear tree, and a cellar that the previous owner used to keep a mule in. It has been kept for you for nine years by someone who loved it. It is a home, and it will be, you suspect, a target.
* Rooms above the firm in Lime Street, discreet and dull, and near the money
  $ b5.haven = "rooms"
  $ standing += 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  It is the cautious thing. It is the right thing for a banker, and the wrong thing for anyone else. Mr. Hale will be astonished to find that his partner has taken the second floor, and has curtains.
?if coven_path() and can_acquire("coven_house")
  ~> family_house
?elif can_acquire("hidden_chamber")
  $ do acquire("hidden_chamber")
?end
~> tasks
