# court
## The card
$ do discover("the_london_covens")
$ do meet("marchmont")
You have a good evening coat, and a hired carriage, and an engraved card. It is a large house in a square in Mayfair, white stucco, with a canopy and a lamp and a footman in plum livery who stands in the door with the face of a man who has been expecting someone else.
Lady Marchmont receives in a long drawing room at the back, with the curtains drawn and a very good fire, and eleven people in it, none of whom breathe. She is small and white and straight, in gray silk, with a great deal of lace and an old, plain, penetrating face, and she sits in a high chair without any ceremony.
"Mr. Collins," she says. "Of the sixth line. I had thought that the line was a legend. I have been wrong about a number of things, and it is always a pleasure to find a new one."
The room has gone very still. You are being weighed, and you can feel the scale.
"There is a custom," she says. "A stranger who comes to London is presented to the Court. It is not a law. It is merely very old, and the Court is very fond of it, and has a number of ways of showing its displeasure with those who do not keep it."
:the_court
* Accept the courtesy, and present yourself in form, with the family at your back ?if coven_size() > 0
  $ b5.court = "called"
  $ standing += 2
  $ do discover("the_court_of_lamps")
  She is plainly pleased, and plainly surprised, and covers both with a gracious bend of the head. The room breathes out, in a manner of speaking. She looks past you at the family, and her eyes, which are very old, rest on each of them in turn. "A coven of the sixth blood," she says, softly. "In my drawing room. I did not think that I should live to see it. I wish that I had put on the other gown."
* Accept the courtesy, alone, and say that the family is not yet ready for the Court ?if coven_size() > 0
  $ b5.court = "alone"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  She hears you out, and nods slowly. It is the answer of a man who has something to protect, and she respects it. She says that the door is open, and that it will be open on any night that you are ready to bring them. It is as courteous as a threat can be.
* Accept the courtesy, but say that you will present yourself in your own time
  $ b5.court = "regrets"
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing -= 1
  The room turns, very slightly, as a field of corn does in a wind. Lady Marchmont smiles. It is the smile of a woman who has been defied by better, and has outlived them. "Of course," she says. "In your own time. London has so very much of it."
* Say nothing, and let the family answer ?if coven_size() > 0
  $ b5.court = "sent"
  $ standing += 1
  ~> family_answers
<~

# family_answers
?if in_circle("prisca") and member("prisca", "status") == "active"
  Prisca steps forward, a small gray figure, and curtsies, which is a thing that you have never seen her do, and says in a tone of perfect, level courtesy that the principal is occupied, and that the house of Collins and Hale will do itself the honor of presenting its compliments in form. Lady Marchmont looks at her for a long time. She says that she has met, in a long life, three people who could do that in that tone. She says that Prisca is the fourth.
?elif in_circle("fiammetta") and member("fiammetta", "status") == "active"
  Fiammetta steps forward, small and quick and dark, and says in a clear voice that the principal is occupied, and that the family will be honored to call, and that in the meantime they should be very grateful for a cup of tea. It is quite the wrong thing to say, and exactly right. Lady Marchmont laughs, once, like a bell, and sends for the tea.
?else
  {_reader_name} steps forward, and bows, and says with great simplicity that the principal is occupied, and that the family is honored. It is not a clever speech. It is a plain, sincere one, and the room responds to it better than to any cleverness.
?end
